|~ qlang operand catalog — CLI format family. Pure value-to-String
   formatters the CLI binds alongside the io operands. Each takes a
   pipeValue subject and returns a rendered String; chaining them
   in front of `@out` is how a query produces stdout. JS impls live
   in `cli/src/format-operands.mjs`, plain functions over the values
   the head of each verb checks [D80]. Each verb sits directly under
   the per-site error tags it raises. ~|

:pretty
  |~~ Renders any value as its canonical qlang-literal display
      form — the same bytes `printValue` produces. Round-trips
      through `parse | apply /` back to the same value, so
      `pretty | parse | apply / | pretty` is an identity for any
      qlang-shaped subject.

      ~(42 | pretty | eq "42")
      ~("hi" | pretty | eq "\"hi\"")
      ~([1 2 3] | pretty | eq "[1 2 3]")
      ~({:a 1} | pretty | eq "{:a 1}")
      ~(:foo | pretty | eq ":foo")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :cli/format/pretty})

:tjson
  |~~ Renders any value as the tagged-JSON wire format from core's
      `codec.mjs`. Round-trips through `parseTjson`, so two qlang
      processes chained over a Unix pipe preserve identity for
      keyword / Set / Map / TaggedInstance / error values that
      plain JSON cannot represent. Use it for qlang-to-qlang IPC;
      use `json` (in `:format` family of core) for talking to
      non-qlang consumers.

      ~(42 | tjson | parseTjson | eq 42)
      ~([1 :foo "hi"] | tjson | parseTjson | eq [1 :foo "hi"])
      ~(#[1 2 3] | tjson | parseTjson | eq #[1 2 3])
      ~({:a 1 :b :keyword} | tjson | parseTjson | eq {:a 1 :b :keyword})
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :cli/format/tjson})

:table
  |~~ Renders a Vec of Maps as a fixed-width tabular string, a view
      for a terminal. Columns are derived from keys in first-occurrence
      order across all rows; missing keys render as empty cells. A
      String cell prints bare, null as an empty cell, and every other
      value as its literal on one line. Empty Vec yields the marker
      "(empty)".

      ~([{:name "alice" :age 30} {:name "bob" :age 25}] | table | contains "alice")
      ~([{:at {:file "A.java" :line 12}}] | table | contains "{:file \"A.java\" :line 12}")
      ~([] | table | eq "(empty)")
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:subject ::vec | :returns ::string | ::builtin{:impl :cli/format/table})

::TableSubjectNotVecError
  |~~ Subject of `table` must be Vec.
      ~(
        42 | table
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::TableSubjectNotVecError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

::TableRowNotMapError
  |~~ Element of `table` input collection must be Map.
      ~(
        [42 "hello"] | table
        !| [type /index /actualType]
        |  eq [::TableRowNotMapError 0 ::number]
       )
  ~~|
